<h2>A short order with a durable lesson</h2>
<p>The judgment is brief — the kind of oral order that decides most bail dockets — but it crystallises how Delhi courts actually handle the two arguments the prosecution makes in nearly every intermediate-quantity NDPS case: the seriousness of a ten-year maximum, and the accused&rsquo;s other involvements.</p>

<h2>Argument one: &ldquo;punishable with up to ten years&rdquo;</h2>
<p>Section 21(b) NDPS Act carries rigorous imprisonment which may extend to ten years for intermediate-quantity contraband. Prosecutors press the ceiling; the bail court looks at the floor and the facts. Two features mattered here:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Position in the band.</strong> At 10.21 grams against a 5-gram small-quantity mark and a 250-gram commercial mark, the recovery sat near the bottom of the intermediate band — &ldquo;nearing the small quantity&rdquo;, in the Court&rsquo;s words. Within-band position is a legitimate, frequently decisive consideration.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No Section 37 bar.</strong> Below commercial quantity, the twin conditions never engage. The maximum sentence informs gravity but does not import a presumption against bail.</div>
</div>

<h2>Argument two: &ldquo;involved in three more cases&rdquo;</h2>
<p>Criminal antecedents are relevant to the risk of reoffending and the credibility of the clean-record argument. But the label &ldquo;involved&rdquo; conceals three very different things, and courts increasingly insist on the distinction:</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Status of the other case</th><th>Weight at the bail stage</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Conviction</td><td>Full weight — a proved propensity, especially for like offences</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Pending trial</td><td>Moderate and contextual — an accusation, not a finding; nature and stage matter</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Acquittal</td><td>Little to none — the involvement argument collapses once the State&rsquo;s own process has exonerated the accused</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Here, acquittals in two of three cited cases — including the gravest — left the State&rsquo;s antecedents plank with little to stand on. The decision aligns with the broader principle that bail is not to be withheld as punishment, and that each application turns on the cumulative effect of its own circumstances.</p>

<h2>Practice pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Chart the antecedents yourself.</strong> Do not let &ldquo;involved in X cases&rdquo; pass unparticularised: obtain the FIR numbers, stages and outcomes, and place acquittal judgments on record.</p>
  <p><strong>Locate the recovery within the band.</strong> An intermediate-quantity case at 2x the small threshold argues very differently from one at 0.9x the commercial threshold.</p>
  <p><strong>Ask for direct jail transmission.</strong> A direction that the order go straight to the Jail Superintendent saves days between order and release.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises a judgment of the Delhi High Court for general information. It is not legal advice; bail outcomes in NDPS matters depend on quantity, recovery and the accused&rsquo;s record.</div>
